# Line endings.
#
# Daps is developed on Windows but everything it produces runs on Linux — the
# toolkit container and the remote host. A shell script with CRLF endings fails
# the moment it runs there:
#
#   /tmp/provision-generic-vps.sh: line 1: set: pipefail: invalid option name
#
# Git Bash tolerates CRLF, so the failure only ever appears on the far side.
#
# This file keeps LF endings on the files in THIS repo. It cannot reach a user's
# project repos, which are separate repositories in sibling directories — those
# are handled at the boundary instead, by
# ConfigUtils.CopyFileAndReplaceLineEndingsForLinux in the deploy and provision
# executors. The two mechanisms cover different ground; neither replaces the other.
#
# Note for template files: `dapsman init` copies templates/ on the workstation, so
# pinning them to LF here is what stops a new project being born with CRLF scripts.

* text=auto

# Executed on Linux, or read by tools that are strict about endings.
*.sh            text eol=lf
*.bash          text eol=lf
*.yaml          text eol=lf
*.yml           text eol=lf
*.caddy         text eol=lf
Caddyfile       text eol=lf
*.dockerfile    text eol=lf
Dockerfile      text eol=lf

# Secret files are bind-mounted into containers and read as credentials; a
# trailing CR would silently become part of the value.
*.txt           text eol=lf

# Binary — never touch.
*.tar           binary
*.tar.gz        binary
*.png           binary
*.jpg           binary
*.jpeg          binary
*.gif           binary
*.ico           binary
*.woff          binary
*.woff2         binary
